They run on the repository as you leave it. `npm run typecheck`, `npm run lint`,
`npm run format`, and `npm test` run the same four locally.

### The build interface

`npm ci` followed by `npm run build` produces the complete static site into
`dist/` at the repository root, with an `index.html` at the root of that
directory as the entry point. The scripts that do this are already in
`package.json`.

That directory runs correctly when served as-is from a static file server, both
at the server root and under a sub-path of it, so every URL the running game
requests resolves relative to the page rather than the origin root. The art
under `assets/` is loaded that way, as `specs/assets.md` states.

## Goal of this build

Produce a complete, polished, playable game that runs entirely in a browser.
This is a substantial front-end build: real-time rendered graphics, a
fixed-timestep simulation, a fog-of-war visibility system, tile-locked maze
movement, three distinct predator behaviors, several screens, and a HUD. Aim for
a game a person would enjoy playing, tense and readable.

### Hard requirements

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
- Write the runtime the game stands on: the frame loop and its delta time, the
  canvas fit, keyboard input, image loading, audio, and the debug overlay.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
- Build on the {{engine.name}} engine, taking the frame loop, the input actions,
  audio, asset loading, and the debug overlay from it.
{{else}}
- Build on the {{engine.name}} engine, taking the frame loop, rendering, the
  input actions, audio, asset loading, and the debug overlay from it, and
  writing the game inside its framework of game modes, actors, components, and
  controllers.
{{/if}}
- Render real graphics on the canvas rather than an ASCII or text-only
  rendering: the creatures, the maze tiles, and the flare from the art seeded
  under `assets/`, which `specs/assets.md` states in full, and everything else
  as shapes and text drawn in code.
- Run in the browser with no backend. Everything needed to play ships with the
  build, nothing is fetched from another origin at runtime, and no API key or
  credential is needed to build, run, or play.
{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
- Hold the game's whole state in one value, the one the frame loop advances and
  the debug surface reads.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
- Hold the game's whole state in `FathomState`, as `specs/state.md` declares it.
{{else}}
- Hold the game's whole authoritative state in `FathomState`, the world's game
  state, as `specs/state.md` declares it.
{{/if}}
- Expose the debugging and automation surface `specs/instrumentation.md` fixes,
  over the fixed-step, deterministic core it requires.
- Replace the starter `README.md` with one explaining what the game is, how to
  install dependencies, how to run it in development, how to produce the static
  production build, and the controls.

## Code quality

The code is fit for a long-lived codebase shared with human developers. The
type-check, lint, and format checks pass, and unit tests cover the simulation.

## Coordinate system and presentation

All positions, sizes, speeds, and ranges in this specification are given in the
game's own logical units on a fixed stage of `STAGE_W x STAGE_H` (`1280 x 720`,
16:9). The origin `(0, 0)` is the top-left; `x` increases to the right and `y`
increases downward.

- `STAGE_W x STAGE_H` is the game's logical design size. Fitting it to the
  browser window is the runtime's: the uniform scale that preserves the aspect
  ratio, the letterboxed centering, and the device pixel ratio. The complete
  stage is therefore on screen at every window size, on load and at any pixel
  density.
- The letterbox bars around the stage carry the stage's background color.
- Draw in logical units, and take the canvas element's own size from the runtime
  alone.
- Game logic operates in logical units, independent of the rendered scale.

Every rate in this specification is per second and every duration is in seconds.

### The tile grid

The maze is laid out on a tile grid of `GRID_COLS` (`36`) columns by `GRID_ROWS`
(`18`) rows, each tile `TILE` (`32`) logical units square. Column `0`'s left edge
is at `GRID_ORIGIN_X` (`64`) and row `0`'s top edge is at `GRID_ORIGIN_Y` (`80`),
so the maze region spans `x` in `[64, 1216]` and `y` in `[80, 656]`.

| Quantity | Value |
| --- | --- |
| The left edge of column `tx` | `GRID_ORIGIN_X + tx * TILE` |
| The top edge of row `ty` | `GRID_ORIGIN_Y + ty * TILE` |
| The center of tile `(tx, ty)` | `(GRID_ORIGIN_X + tx * TILE + TILE / 2, GRID_ORIGIN_Y + ty * TILE + TILE / 2)` |

Tile centers are the reference points for movement and for sensing throughout
this specification. The HUD occupies the strip above the maze region, `y` in
`[0, 80]`, and the strip below it, `y` in `[656, 720]`.

## The screens

The game is a state machine over seven screens: the title screen and its menu,
the how-to-play screen, the dive countdown, live play, the paused screen, the
maze-cleared interstitial, and the game-over screen. Every build carries all
seven. `specs/state.md` names the value each one carries in `screen`, and
`specs/ui.md` defines what each one shows, what its controls do, and how the
game moves between them.

## Visual design

The look is bioluminescence in the abyss: cold light glowing out of a near-black
maze. The palette, the glow, the layout of each screen, and every other aspect
of the look are yours, within the requirements below.

The creatures, the maze tiles, and the flare bloom are seeded as art under
`assets/` and are rendered from it. The forager's light, the plankton, the sonar
wavefronts, the ink, the HUD, and all text are drawn in code. `specs/assets.md`
states which is which.

Whatever look you choose, a player reads all of the following at a glance.

| What a player reads | Requirement |
| --- | --- |
| The dark | An unrevealed tile is drawn as flat darkness, near-black and no brighter than a tenth of full brightness, hiding whether it is rock or open water. |
| Rock and water | Where the game shows a revealed tile, rock and open corridor are told apart at a glance. |
| The forager | The forager stands clearly apart from the corridor it swims along, and the light it carries reads as a cool glow around it. |
| The predators | Each of the three predators is told apart from the other two and from the forager. |
| The amber lights | The Lanternjaw's bulb and the bonus drifter are each drawn as a warm amber light, red-leaning and clearly warmer than the water, the rock, and the forager's own light. The two read as near-identical lights, so one is not told from the other at a distance. |
| Plankton | A plankton reads as a small point of light against the corridor floor it sits on. |
| The wavefronts | A sonar wavefront is drawn as it travels, and the three tints `specs/state.md` names are told apart from each other: cyan for the forager's pulse, violet for an ordinary Gloamfin ping, and orange for the Gloamfin's lost-you ping. |
| The ink | An ink cloud is visibly darker than the water it stands in, and covers the area `specs/sensing.md` gives it. |
| Text | Text is legible against its background at the logical stage size. |

Every screen's text is set in a monospace type family, taken from a system stack
rather than from a font the page downloads, so the game renders the same with no
network.
